# Harbinger Environment Configuration
# Copy to .env and fill in your values

# ── AI Analysis Provider ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# claude    (default) - ssh to the machine holding a Claude CLI session and run
#                       `claude -p` on the Claude Max subscription. No API key,
#                       no per-token billing.
# ollama              - local Ollama
# anthropic           - paid Anthropic Messages API (legacy tool-use path)
ANALYSIS_PROVIDER=claude

# Claude CLI over SSH (used when ANALYSIS_PROVIDER=claude).
# Required — no default, so a host is never baked into this public repo.
# CLAUDE_CLI_SSH_HOST=user@your-host
# CLAUDE_CLI_BIN=/opt/homebrew/bin/claude
# CLAUDE_CLI_TIMEOUT_MS=900000
#
# CLAUDE_CLI_MODEL — accepts an alias (haiku / sonnet / opus) or a full model
# name (e.g. claude-haiku-4-5). Aliases always track the current generation, so
# they do not need updating when a new model ships.
#
# Frontier models are NOT needed here. Harbinger pre-fetches all the IOC data
# and enrichment itself, so the model is summarizing supplied facts rather than
# reasoning from scratch. Measured on a Log4Shell triage prompt:
#   haiku   ~7s   cheapest, adequate for chat and routine triage
#   sonnet  ~4s   default — best quality-per-token for briefs
#   opus    ~8s   reserve for the weekly strategic synthesis, if at all
# Start with sonnet; drop chat to haiku if you want to conserve subscription
# usage. All three draw on the Claude Max subscription, not per-token billing.
CLAUDE_CLI_MODEL=sonnet

# Working directory for the remote CLI session. Defaults to /tmp.
# Do NOT point this at a home directory: the CLI walks up from its cwd
# collecting CLAUDE.md files, which pulls the operator's personal global
# instructions and memory into every threat-intel prompt. Combined with
# --setting-sources project (always passed), /tmp yields a clean session.
# CLAUDE_CLI_CWD=/tmp
#
# The CLI session runs with ALL tools disallowed. Harbinger gathers related
# IOCs, Wazuh exposure, and KEV/EPSS itself (prefetch.ts) and injects them into
# prompt, so analysis stays deterministic and the model gets no shell.

# Internal domain suffixes that must NEVER be sent to third-party enrichment
# APIs (VirusTotal, Shodan, AbuseIPDB, URLScan...). RFC1918, loopback,
# link-local, CGNAT, and *.local/.lan/.corp/.internal are always blocked in
# code. Add comma-separated suffixes here for internal names that live on a
# PUBLIC registrable domain (e.g. .home.example.com), which the TLD check
# cannot catch on its own.
# INTERNAL_DOMAIN_SUFFIXES=.home.example.com,.lab.example.net

# Anthropic API key — ONLY needed when ANALYSIS_PROVIDER=anthropic
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Abuse.ch Auth Key (for URLhaus + ThreatFox feeds)
# Register free at https://auth.abuse.ch/
ABUSE_CH_AUTH_KEY=

# Feed poll interval in milliseconds (default: 1 hour)
# POLL_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# Database path (default: ./harbinger.db)
# DB_PATH=./harbinger.db

# Server port (default: 4001)
# PORT=4001

# Vite dev server port (default: 5174). Override per host if you need a
# different port — manage.sh sources .env to read this.
# CLIENT_PORT=5174

# ── Environment exposure: Wazuh vulnerability inventory ─────────────────────
# Answers "do I actually HAVE this CVE?" from the Wazuh vulnerability detector,
# rather than only "is this CVE bad in general". This is what makes a brief
# actionable: affected host, package, installed version, and the fix condition.
#
# Read-only: _search against wazuh-states-vulnerabilities* through the
# dashboard console proxy. The raw indexer port is not used.
# Unset = Harbinger skips the lookup and says so; it never guesses.
# WAZUH_DASHBOARD_URL=https://your-wazuh-host
# WAZUH_DASHBOARD_USER=admin
# WAZUH_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=

# Exploitation signal (CISA KEV + FIRST.org EPSS) needs no configuration and
# no API key — Harbinger calls both public endpoints directly. KEV is cached
# for 6 hours. Together with the Wazuh lookup: present + in KEV = patch now.

